WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.460
The first film timed the knee jerk, leg by leg.

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.760
Now you step on a tack: is lifting your foot faster or slower, and why?

3
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:15.013
Put the two reflexes side by side.

4
00:00:16.233 --> 00:00:22.233
Both use the same five stations: receptor, sensory neuron, integrating centre, motor

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.973
neuron, effector. Their circuits differ in the middle.

6
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:29.253
Their fibres and path lengths differ too.

7
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.973
In the knee jerk, the spindle's sensory fibre synapses straight onto the motor

8
00:00:34.973 --> 00:00:38.633
neuron. That is one synapse in the spinal cord.

9
00:00:39.893 --> 00:00:42.193
This direct path is called monosynaptic.

10
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:48.197
But a side branch excites an interneuron, a neuron that stays inside the cord.

11
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.657
It quiets the hamstring, the muscle that would oppose the kick.

12
00:00:53.937 --> 00:00:55.857
So even the knee jerk is not one wire.

13
00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.310
Now the tack. Pain-sensing endings in the skin fire.

14
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:07.270
Their thin sensory fibres excite a chain of interneurons in the cord, right from the

15
00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.270
start.

16
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.000
Some interneurons drive the flexors, which lift the foot.

17
00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:15.260
Others quiet the opposing extensors.

18
00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.460
Charles Sherrington showed this pairing in mammals, and called it reciprocal

19
00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.460
innervation.

20
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.420
Now run both timers together, on the same scale.

21
00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.060
The withdrawal bar keeps growing after the knee jerk's has stopped.

22
00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.800
It has more synapses, so it shows more ticks.

23
00:01:34.933 --> 00:01:39.953
In this example the withdrawal has three synapses, and the knee jerk has only one.

24
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:44.073
So are the extra synapses most of the extra time?

25
00:01:50.517 --> 00:01:56.097
No. In this model, each synapse adds only about a millisecond.

26
00:01:57.177 --> 00:01:59.297
The big difference is the sensory leg.

27
00:02:00.797 --> 00:02:04.737
Thin pain fibres conduct slowly, so that leg takes tens of milliseconds.

28
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.740
Why is a thin fibre slow?

29
00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.740
A thin axon offers more resistance to the current spreading ahead of the impulse.

30
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:18.980
So the impulse travels more slowly, and the sensory leg stretches out.

31
00:02:22.533 --> 00:02:25.513
Part of the gap is also the longer motor path.

32
00:02:26.753 --> 00:02:29.853
In this example, slow fibres are most of it, not all.

33
00:02:31.093 --> 00:02:35.053
In real people, fibre speed and processing in the cord both vary.

34
00:02:36.117 --> 00:02:40.977
Both reflexes end on skeletal muscle, and that makes both of them somatic.

35
00:02:42.257 --> 00:02:43.917
Somatic does not mean voluntary.

36
00:02:45.477 --> 00:02:47.137
Neither reflex waits for a decision.

37
00:02:48.267 --> 00:02:51.807
So in these two reflexes, the synapses set the circuit.

38
00:02:53.147 --> 00:02:55.827
The slow fibres set most of the extra time.

39
00:02:57.047 --> 00:02:58.947
The sum from the first film still holds.
